      "most_cephalopods_are_opportunistic_car": " — most cephalopods are opportunistic carnivores that can switch prey easily.",
      "most_coastal_octopuses_are_nocturnal_h": "Most coastal octopuses are nocturnal, hunting between dusk and dawn. Their large pupils (proportional to body size, even larger than human) and W-shaped pupil aperture optimize for low-light visibility.",
      "most_cognitive_tests_were_designed_for": " Most cognitive tests were designed for primates first, then adapted to other animals. Mirror tests, Y-mazes, tool-shaping puzzles — these all assume a centralized brain making centralized decisions. Cephalopod cognition keeps PARTIALLY passing tests it was never designed for. The honest interpretation: we don't yet have good tests for distributed-arm cognition or swarm intelligence. ",
      "most_common_jellyfish_in_temperate_sea": "Most common jellyfish in temperate seas. Their sting is too weak to seriously injure humans, but it irritates eyes + mucous membranes.",
      "most_cultures_don_t_question_eating_ce": "Most cultures don't question eating cephalopods — they're part of generations of cuisine. But the recent recognition of cephalopod sentience (UK 2021, EU 2010, multiple scientific declarations) has surfaced a question many find newly difficult:",
      "most_marine_biodiversity_hot_spots_are": "Most marine biodiversity hot spots are rocky substrates — they offer more crevice habitat per unit area than sand.",
      "most_marine_populations_are_declining": "Most marine populations are declining. ",
      "most_octopuses_are_nocturnal_or_crepus": "Most octopuses are nocturnal or crepuscular. Their eyes have W-shaped pupils that allow them to see in low light and discriminate polarized light (though they may be color-blind).",
      "most_octopuses_are_semelparous_they_re": "Most octopuses are semelparous — they reproduce ONCE and then die. The female brood her eggs continuously without eating until they hatch.",
      "most_octopuses_are_solitary_but_at_lea": "Most octopuses are SOLITARY, but at least 4 species are now documented as facultatively social — including Octopus tetricus in Jervis Bay, where 16+ individuals share a single artificial reef.",
      "most_octopuses_live_1_2_years_they_mat": "Most octopuses live 1-2 years. They mate once, brood eggs without eating, then die. Semelparous reproduction is the cephalopod default.",
