      "print_sports": "Print + Sports",
      "print_spread_across_europe_with_a_spee": "Print spread across Europe with a speed that looks miraculous unless you know the specific event that started it. In 1462, Mainz was caught in a local feudal conflict (the Diocesan War, also called the Mainz Archbishopric Feud) between two rival claimants to the archbishop's seat. The city was sacked and burned. The print shops were ruined.",
      "print_spread_from_mainz_outward_in_wav": "Print spread from Mainz outward in waves. Each major center developed a specialty matched to its trade, religion, and politics. By 1500 these four cities defined what European printing looked like.",
      "print_this_module_as_a_classroom_hando": "Print this module as a classroom handout",
      "print_this_module_page": "Print this module page",
      "print_tourism": "Print + Tourism",
      "print_war": "Print + War",
      "print_without_galileo_s_name_hidden_im": "Print without Galileo's name, hidden imprint",
      "printing_is_the_wonder_of_all_wonders_": "Printing is the wonder of all wonders. It is the gift of God, by which old things may be made new and new things made known.",
      "printing_made_the_pages_but_a_stack_of": "Printing made the pages — but a stack of paper is not a book. Fold (folio, quarto, octavo), gather, sew, bind. Interactive simulator of the bindery work that followed every print run.",
      "printingpress": "📜 PrintingPress",
      "printingpress_an_interactive_printing_": "PrintingPress: an interactive printing-press history and simulation tool. AlloFlow STEAM Lab (Pomeranz, A., ",
      "printingpress_error": "PrintingPress error: ",
      "printingpress_is_part_of_alloflow_crea": "PrintingPress is part of AlloFlow, created by Dr. Aaron Pomeranz (school psychologist) for accessible + universal-design-for-learning-aligned educational tools. The tool serves K-12 + adult learners studying print history, typography, book arts, publishing, + adjacent cultural fields. The tool is open-source within the AlloFlow ecosystem + freely available to educators + students.",
      "project_gutenberg": "Project Gutenberg",
      "proofreader_s_eye": "🔍 Proofreader’s eye",
      "proportional_reasoning_and_functions_p": " Proportional reasoning and functions (pamphlet reach calculator + mechanical-advantage screw math).",
      "public_libraries": "Public libraries",
      "publick_occurrences": "Publick Occurrences",
      "publisher_or_editor_in_chief": "Publisher or editor-in-chief",
      "pull_any_hardback_off_a_shelf_look_at_": "Pull any hardback off a shelf. Look at the title page, the colophon (back of the title page), the type, the leading. You now know what every choice means.",
      "pull_the_bar_watch_the_screw_turn_the_": "Pull the bar, watch the screw turn, the platen drop, the type ink the paper. Mechanical advantage, force, and historical accuracy. The wow tile.",
      "pulling_the_bar_250_times_a_day_every_": "Pulling the bar 250 times a day, every working day, year after year, wore out shoulders and elbows. By age 50 many pressmen had lost the strength for full-day work. The Linotype operator inherited the same kind of injury in a different shape (keyboard) 400 years later.",
      "pulp_cover_art": "Pulp Cover Art",
      "pulp_era": "Pulp era",
      "pulp_era_tolkien_modern_hugo_nebula_aw": "Pulp era → Tolkien → modern. Hugo + Nebula awards. The genre history.",
      "pulp_fiction_era": "Pulp Fiction Era",
      "punch": "Punch",
      "punch_matrix_cast_the_three_step_proce": "Punch → matrix → cast. The three-step process that made identical reusable type possible. Type-metal alloy: lead + tin + antimony, and why each.",
      "punk_zines": "Punk zines",
      "pure_white_linen_rags_bedsheets_shirts": "Pure white linen rags — bedsheets, shirts. Best fiber, highest yield, premium paper.",
      "puritan_reformer_sermons": "Puritan + Reformer sermons",
      "pursue_openly_accept_consequences": "Pursue openly + accept consequences",
      "quarto_4": "Quarto (4°)",
      "question": "Question ",
      "quick_reference_for_vocabulary_used_th": "Quick reference for vocabulary used throughout the tool. Filter by typing.",
      "quietly_take_a_small_share_of_the_pape": "Quietly take a small share of the paper sales",
      "qwerty_designed_for_typewriters_in_187": "QWERTY (designed for typewriters in 1873) was not frequency-ordered; it was designed to slow typists down so the keys would not jam. ETAOIN SHRDLU represents a counterfactual: if the dominant keyboard had descended from Linotype rather than typewriter, your laptop today would feel completely different under your fingers.",
      "radio_scripts": "Radio scripts",
      "radio_scripts_radio_program_guides_the": "Radio scripts, radio program guides, the radio-magazine industry, BBC + NPR print presence.",
      "rag_paper_held_the_market_for_600_year": "Rag paper held the market for ~600 years (1276 Fabriano to ~1880). The next paper revolution was wood-pulp papermaking — invented in stages by Friedrich Gottlob Keller (Germany, 1844, ground wood) and Hugh Burgess + Charles Watt (Britain + USA, 1854, chemical pulping). Wood pulp was vastly cheaper than rag, kicked off the mass-circulation newspaper era of 1870-1920, and made paper price collapse another order of magnitude. Maine's paper-mill industry (Sappi, Verso) descends from this wood-pulp tradition.",
      "rag_pickers_rag_and_bone_men_collect_w": "Rag pickers + rag-and-bone-men collect worn-out linen, hemp, and cotton textiles from households, hospitals, and ships. Linen is best — long, strong fibers. Wool is useless (felts when beaten). Quality is sorted at collection — white linen separately from coarse hemp.",
      "rag_piles_paper_mills_were_repeatedly_": "Rag piles + paper mills were repeatedly identified as cholera + typhus vectors during 19th-century epidemics. The 1832 cholera outbreak in Europe spread along rag-collection routes; the 1854 London Broad Street outbreak had multiple sources, but rag mills were among the suspects (later research confirmed water, not rags).",
      "rag_quality": "Rag quality:",
      "rags_are_sorted_cut_and_soaked_in_lime": "Rags are sorted, cut, and soaked in lime water for days to weeks (\"retting\") to break down sizing + soften fibers. Then boiled in lye for several hours to remove dirt + lignin. The boiling smell of a 15th-century paper mill was famously terrible — half the reason mills were built downstream of towns.",
      "rare_book_libraries": "Rare-book libraries",
      "read_a_primary_source": "📜 Read a primary source",
      "readable": "Readable",
      "reader_gives_up_the_text_physically_ex": "Reader gives up. The text physically exists but does not function.",
      "readers_per_copy": "Readers per copy: ",
      "reading_a_book": "Reading a book",
      "reading_mirror_reversed_type_by_candle": "Reading mirror-reversed type by candle or oil-lamp light, for 10+ hour days, ruined eyes. Reading glasses became common among compositors a century before they were common in the general population.",
      "reading_old_papers": "Reading old papers",
      "real_history_luther_s_95_theses_oct_15": "Real history: Luther’s 95 Theses (Oct 1517) reached Rome by Dec 1517. Distance ~750 miles. The mechanism was exactly this multiplier — print + read-aloud + reprint, in dozens of cities, all in three months. No army or institution could keep up with the spread.",
